{
  "id": "COMPOUND:24022 - PROTEIN:5814",
  "verbalization": "Miyabenol A is shown to have a target relationship with VMRC-MELG through multiple pathways, with the most direct link being its interaction with 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14. This subunit is a known target of the compound, and its interaction with VMRC-MELG is supported by multiple evidence chains. The compound also targets Ataxin-2, which is a protein involved in cellular processes that may be relevant to VMRC-MELG's function. The presence of a common target, (1R,3R,4S,7S,9S,11S,13R,14R,15R)-4,11,13,14-tetrahydroxy-7-[(2Z,4R)-4-(hydroxymethyl)hex-2-en-2-yl]-3,9,15-trimethoxy-12,12-dimethyl-6,17-dioxabicyclo[11.3.1]heptadecan-5-one, further strengthens the connection between Miyabenol A and VMRC-MELG. The evidence suggests that Miyabenol A modulates the proteasome system, which is a key pathway in protein degradation and cellular homeostasis. The mechanism of action is supported by the presence of a known target and the compound's ability to interact with multiple components of the proteasome pathway. The integration of these pathways indicates that Miyabenol A may exert its effects through the regulation of proteasome activity, which in turn could influence VMRC-MELG's function. The evidence is consistent with a mechanistic link, though the exact role of VMRC-MELG in this context remains to be fully elucidated.",
